{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"1793697922677","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"cc4","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Inspect Chrome MCP configuration. Record exact localhost endpoint, Chrome executable, and dedicated test profile path. Do not assume port `9222`.\n\n2. Probe configured endpoint’s `/json/version`. If unavailable, inspect Chrome processes and port ownership. Distinguish missing debug listener from wrong port or occupied port.\n\n3. Keep personal Chrome windows open. Do not terminate personal processes, reuse personal profile data, delete profile locks, or change permissions.\n\n4. Launch installed Chrome with authorized dedicated test profile and configured port. Windows Bash example—replace placeholders with verified values:\n   ```bash\n   \"/c/path/to/chrome.exe\" \\\n     --user-data-dir=\"C:/path/to/dedicated-test-profile\" \\\n     --remote-debugging-address=127.0.0.1 \\\n     --remote-debugging-port=<configured-port>\n   ```\n   Use a separate, non-default user-data directory; `--profile-directory` alone does not isolate it. If another process owns the port, do not kill it or silently change configuration; report conflict.\n\n5. Wait for startup, then probe `/json/version` again. Require valid browser metadata and `webSocketDebuggerUrl`. If still unavailable, inspect launch errors and actual command line. Retry only after identifying and fixing the cause within existing authorization.\n\n6. Reconnect installed Chrome MCP using its configured connection. List pages and verify intended test profile/page before further actions. Endpoint response alone does not prove MCP readiness.\n\n7. If blocked by policy, permissions, or required configuration changes outside authorization, report exact blocker and request approval. No reinstall, elevation, OAuth, or personal-browser shutdown."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session